  <div class="section-label">The Inland Port Nexus</div>
  <h2>SR-177 and the Western Weber Inland Port</h2>

  <p>The relationship between SR-177 and the Western Weber Inland Port is a significant and contested dimension of this project. UDOT frames the highway primarily as a commuter corridor serving the 40%+ of western Weber County workers who commute to other counties. However, the highway's design — 65 mph limited-access, four lanes, grade-separated interchanges — is equally capable of handling heavy truck freight.</p>

  <p>UDOT frames SR-177 primarily as a commuter corridor serving the 40%+ of western Weber County workers who commute to other counties.{% cite "udot-ses-build" %} Public commenters, including a letter published in the Standard-Examiner (April 24, 2026),{% cite "se-opposition-letter" %} have raised concerns that the highway's design may facilitate semitruck traffic to the Western Weber Inland Port. Wasatch Front Regional Council's 2023–2050 Regional Transportation Plan{% cite "wfrc-rtp-2023-2050" %} links SR-177 to the "Wasatch Choice Vision" framework,{% cite "wfrc-wasatch-choice" %} which coordinates transportation with land-use and economic development, including industrial expansion in western Weber County. Whether freight is a primary design driver or an incidental capability remains an open question in the public record.</p>

